WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.195
Day 21 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.

00:00:04.745 --> 00:00:09.623
Testing RAG: retrieval, faithfulness and answer, separately.

00:00:10.173 --> 00:00:15.038
How to test a RAG system in three layers so that, when it fails, you know whether

00:00:15.038 --> 00:00:18.582
the problem is retrieval, generation or the knowledge base.

00:00:19.882 --> 00:00:24.958
A RAG system answers questions by first searching your documents and then generating

00:00:24.958 --> 00:00:26.711
an answer with what it found.

00:00:27.011 --> 00:00:30.833
When it answers badly, the usual reaction is to tweak the prompt.

00:00:31.133 --> 00:00:32.599
Sometimes that works.

00:00:32.899 --> 00:00:37.618
Often it doesn't, because the problem came earlier: the right document never reached

00:00:37.618 --> 00:00:40.483
the model, and no instruction can make up for that.

00:00:41.783 --> 00:00:46.992
Testing a RAG end to end, with a single metric, mixes two different systems: a

00:00:46.992 --> 00:00:48.995
search engine and a generator.

00:00:49.295 --> 00:00:53.641
This guide splits them into three layers measured separately, and adds negative

00:00:53.641 --> 00:00:57.657
cases, where the correct answer is admitting the information isn't there.

00:00:57.957 --> 00:01:02.756
The result is a diagnosis that says where to fix things, not just that something is

00:01:02.756 --> 00:01:03.103
wrong.

00:01:04.403 --> 00:01:06.101
To follow this guide you need:

00:01:06.651 --> 00:01:12.319
Python 3.10+ and a RAG app exposing the answer and the retrieved chunks.

00:01:12.869 --> 00:01:16.238
20 questions with expected answer and source document.

00:01:16.788 --> 00:01:22.172
DeepEval defaults to an OpenAI model as judge (needs OPENAI_API_KEY); its docs

00:01:22.172 --> 00:01:24.036
explain how to use another.

00:01:25.336 --> 00:01:26.615
This is the setup.

00:01:26.915 --> 00:01:31.132
Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.

00:01:32.432 --> 00:01:34.699
And this is the next setup file.

00:01:35.999 --> 00:01:37.035
Step 1.

00:01:37.335 --> 00:01:39.184
Layer one: retrieval.

00:01:39.734 --> 00:01:44.371
Layer 1, retrieval: for each question, check whether the right document shows up

00:01:44.371 --> 00:01:45.936
among the retrieved chunks.

00:01:46.236 --> 00:01:48.700
It's deterministic and needs no model.

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:53.270
For each question in the dataset, note which document holds the answer.

00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:57.636
The test checks whether that document shows up among the retrieved chunks.

00:01:57.936 --> 00:02:03.420
No model is needed to evaluate it: it's a comparison of identifiers, deterministic

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:04.022
and fast.

00:02:04.322 --> 00:02:06.670
This metric is the system's ceiling.

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:11.145
If the right chunk shows up for seven questions in ten, the final answer can't be

00:02:11.145 --> 00:02:13.207
right with grounding in more than seven.

00:02:13.507 --> 00:02:16.691
Starting here avoids weeks of adjustments in the wrong place.

00:02:17.991 --> 00:02:19.189
Step 2.

00:02:19.489 --> 00:02:21.675
Layer two: faithfulness.

00:02:22.225 --> 00:02:27.120
Layer 2, faithfulness: every claim in the answer must be supported by the retrieved

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:27.533
chunks.

00:02:27.833 --> 00:02:30.146
This is where fabrications get caught.

00:02:30.696 --> 00:02:35.026
Faithfulness asks whether every claim in the answer is supported by the retrieved

00:02:35.026 --> 00:02:35.401
chunks.

00:02:35.701 --> 00:02:40.280
It doesn't evaluate whether the answer is true in general, but whether it comes from

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:41.752
the context the system had.

00:02:42.052 --> 00:02:46.845
This is where fabrications get caught: a fact the model added from prior knowledge,

00:02:46.845 --> 00:02:50.309
an altered figure, a condition the document doesn't mention.

00:02:50.609 --> 00:02:55.152
It's measured with a judge that breaks the answer into claims and verifies each one.

00:02:55.452 --> 00:02:58.554
For figures and dates, add deterministic checks.

00:02:59.854 --> 00:03:00.936
Step 3.

00:03:01.236 --> 00:03:03.131
Layer three: answer.

00:03:03.681 --> 00:03:08.584
Layer 3, answer: does it answer what was asked and match what's expected?

00:03:09.134 --> 00:03:13.936
Only in the third layer do you evaluate whether the answer addresses what was asked

00:03:13.936 --> 00:03:15.557
and matches what's expected.

00:03:15.857 --> 00:03:20.182
An answer can be faithful to the context and still useless: it talks about the

00:03:20.182 --> 00:03:24.230
topic, quotes the document well and doesn't answer the specific question.

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:28.983
Or it can answer correctly from general knowledge without relying on your documents,

00:03:28.983 --> 00:03:30.361
which is a different risk.

00:03:30.661 --> 00:03:34.958
That's why the three layers are read together: each combination of results points to

00:03:34.958 --> 00:03:35.981
a different problem.

00:03:37.281 --> 00:03:38.293
Step 4.

00:03:38.593 --> 00:03:40.059
Negative cases.

00:03:40.609 --> 00:03:44.931
Add negative cases: questions whose answer isn't in the knowledge base.

00:03:45.231 --> 00:03:47.578
The system must say it doesn't know.

00:03:48.128 --> 00:03:51.242
Add questions whose answer isn't in the knowledge base.

00:03:51.542 --> 00:03:56.314
The expected behavior is for the system to say so, instead of assembling a plausible

00:03:56.314 --> 00:03:58.302
answer from vaguely related chunks.

00:03:58.602 --> 00:04:02.965
It's the case that does the most damage in production and the least tested, because

00:04:02.965 --> 00:04:05.594
datasets tend to be built from existing documents.

00:04:05.894 --> 00:04:10.988
Also include nearly covered questions: the document discusses the topic but doesn't

00:04:10.988 --> 00:04:13.443
carry the specific fact being asked for.

00:04:14.743 --> 00:04:15.872
Step 5.

00:04:16.172 --> 00:04:17.649
Diagnose per layer.

00:04:18.199 --> 00:04:23.178
Diagnose per layer: low retrieval is fixed in chunking, index or search; low

00:04:23.178 --> 00:04:25.864
faithfulness is fixed in prompt or model.

00:04:26.164 --> 00:04:28.907
Changing the prompt doesn't fix bad retrieval.

00:04:29.457 --> 00:04:32.850
With the three metrics separated, each fix has its place.

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:36.995
Low retrieval is fixed in chunk size, the index or the search.

00:04:37.295 --> 00:04:41.791
Low faithfulness with good retrieval is fixed in the prompt or by changing models.

00:04:42.091 --> 00:04:46.611
A low answer score with the other two high is usually a format or instructions

00:04:46.611 --> 00:04:47.074
problem.

00:04:47.374 --> 00:04:51.451
The practical rule is not to touch the prompt until you've confirmed retrieval

00:04:51.451 --> 00:04:54.900
works: changing the prompt doesn't fix a search that doesn't find.

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:57.387
Step 6.

00:04:57.687 --> 00:04:59.245
Metrics in CI.

00:04:59.795 --> 00:05:04.548
Bring the three metrics into CI with thresholds and track them on every document

00:05:04.548 --> 00:05:04.964
change.

00:05:05.514 --> 00:05:10.189
Bring the three metrics into the pipeline with a threshold for each, and run the

00:05:10.189 --> 00:05:12.994
eval every time documents change, not just code.

00:05:13.294 --> 00:05:16.431
In a RAG, the knowledge base is part of the system.

00:05:16.731 --> 00:05:21.322
A new document contradicting another, or a folder reorganization, can lower

00:05:21.322 --> 00:05:24.199
retrieval without anyone having touched a line.

00:05:24.499 --> 00:05:29.128
Record how the metrics evolve with each content change so you can locate when a drop

00:05:29.128 --> 00:05:29.459
began.

00:05:30.759 --> 00:05:31.865
An example.

00:05:32.415 --> 00:05:36.225
An internal assistant answers questions about company policies.

00:05:36.525 --> 00:05:40.661
The team builds forty questions with their source document and ten negatives.

00:05:40.961 --> 00:05:47.618
First measurement, with illustrative figures: retrieval 65%, faithfulness 92%,

00:05:47.618 --> 00:05:48.557
answer 60%.

00:05:48.857 --> 00:05:51.530
The team had been tuning the prompt for two weeks.

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:55.991
The numbers show the generator is faithful to what it receives and that the problem

00:05:55.991 --> 00:05:56.743
lies in search.

00:05:57.043 --> 00:05:59.925
They check and find chunks were cutting tables in half.

00:06:00.225 --> 00:06:05.881
They change the chunking strategy and retrieval rises to 90%; the answer score, to

00:06:05.881 --> 00:06:08.157
84%, without touching the prompt.

00:06:08.457 --> 00:06:11.722
On the negatives, three out of ten still make things up.

00:06:12.022 --> 00:06:14.707
That one does get fixed with an instruction.

00:06:16.007 --> 00:06:17.670
The most common mistakes.

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:21.949
Measuring only the final answer and not knowing which layer fails.

00:06:22.499 --> 00:06:25.149
Tuning the prompt when the problem is retrieval.

00:06:25.699 --> 00:06:29.254
Not including questions with no answer in the knowledge base.

00:06:29.804 --> 00:06:33.406
Running the eval only when code changes and not documents.

00:06:34.706 --> 00:06:36.949
To know it is done, check this.

00:06:37.499 --> 00:06:40.091
Each question has its source document noted.

00:06:40.641 --> 00:06:44.150
Retrieval, faithfulness and answer are measured separately.

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:48.151
The dataset includes negative and nearly covered cases.

00:06:48.701 --> 00:06:51.618
The eval runs in CI on document changes.

00:06:52.918 --> 00:06:54.813
And where AI fell short.

00:06:55.363 --> 00:06:58.883
With the right source in context, it still alters figures.

00:06:59.183 --> 00:07:01.519
Faithfulness is measured, not assumed.

00:07:02.819 --> 00:07:04.610
That was day 21.

00:07:04.910 --> 00:07:09.884
The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently

00:07:09.884 --> 00:07:10.892
asked questions.

00:07:11.442 --> 00:07:17.025
